  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA mid-level trough is moving across eastern New \nYork and western New England overnight, ushering in lowering 850mb \ntemps, down to -8C to -12C throughout Tuesday. Boundary layer winds \nare also beginning to veer to the northwest, with current radar \nimagery beginning to show some isolated/scattered lake effect \nrain/snow showers beginning to form over western New York enhanced \nby these winds. The latest model guidance, particularly from the \nRGEM, has begun to show these showers reaching farther east than the \nMohawk Valley and eastern Catskills throughout the early morning \nhours. The Greater Capital District could see some sprinkles, but \nthey will be very short-lived and not lead to any significant \naccumulation.\n\nAfter the rain/snow showers taper off early this morning, high \npressure will build in from the west, bringing much drier air and \nbelow normal temperatures. Some gusty winds will linger in the \nmorning and early afternoon hours, reaching up to 20-25 mph, \nparticularly across western New England, the Capital District, and \nthe eastern Catskills. The dew points in the 40s and 50s observed on \nMonday drop to the teens and 20s by this evening, which will stick \naround into Thursday. Temperatures will be 5 to 10 degrees F below \nnormal, with highs remaining in the 30s and 40s while lows drop into \nthe 20s and teens during the overnight periods Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh confidence in storm system tracking northward along/east of\nthe Appalachians Thursday into Friday, however there remains\nsome uncertainty regarding timing of precipitation onset, as\nsome guidance (EC/EC AIFS) suggests a slower arrival from south\nto north, mainly during Thursday afternoon and evening hours, \nwhile other sources of guidance (GFS/AIGFS) suggest a slightly \nquicker arrival. Given antecedent dry low level air in place, \nP-type could initially be snow/sleet across higher elevations \nmainly above 1000 feet before briefly changing to freezing rain,\nthen rain. Some wet snow/sleet could even briefly mix in at the\nstart across some lower elevations across the upper Hudson \nValley and southern VT. Brief duration should limit overall \nsnow/sleet and ice accumulations and resulting impacts.\n\nAbundant moisture and strong forcing should allow for a period\nof moderate to heavy rain to occur Thursday evening and night.\n01Z/24 NBM 48-hour probs for >1 inch of rainfall is generally\n60-95% across the region, with the exception of portions of the\nwestern Mohawk Valley and eastern portions of the upper Hudson\nValley, where precip shadowing from strong downsloping\neast/southeast winds keep these probs for >1\" generally 35-50%.\nMuch of this rain will fall in a 6-12 hour period, however dry\nantecedent conditions should limit overall river flood threat. \nLatest MMEFS does not indicate any river points exceeding flood\nstage at this time. However, there is a chance that some tidal \nflooding occurs along the Hudson River near Poughkeepsie, so \ntrends will need to be watched. Otherwise, there could be some \nponding of water in some urban/poor drainage areas.\n\nStrong east/southeast winds will be possible across higher\nterrain areas of western New England, the Taconics and the SE \nCatskills, where 01Z/24 NBM 24-hour probs for max wind gusts >40\nmph ending 1 AM Friday are 40-80%. A wind advisory may be needed\nfor portions of these areas, where strong wind gusts of 40-50\nmph could down some trees/tree limbs and result in power\noutages.",
